{
  "id": "ss.relic.pouch",
  "name": "Forager's crop",
  "description": "A pouch of dyed hide, wide and low the way a full bag sits, its mouth open at the top. The cloth round that mouth is gathered into four lobes, and they are laid down *before* the body so that the body covers their lower halves and only their tops clear its outline — the mouth's edge is the bag's own edge gone lumpy, not a rim set on it. Drawn the other way round, each lobe keeps its own ink ring and the rim becomes a string of beads; drawn as a separate bunch above the body, it is a hat with a gap under it. Both were tried, and before them a clean ochre band, which is what made every earlier pass a jar: a smooth rim of revolution over a symmetric belly is ceramic whatever colour it is dyed. Neither side of the body is the other's mirror and the widths carry a little noise, for the same reason. The mouth is a hole and is drawn as one: a linear gradient down the shaft, lit rust at the top where the far wall's inside catches the light, falling through soil to ink where the bag goes deep, with a lit lip of cloth turning down into it so the dark has something bright to be a hole against — rust-on-rust was dark on dark and read as a slot. This turns the shelf's own rule round on purpose: everywhere else a dark ellipse on a bright body is an eye and is banned, and ss.relic.digest, ss.relic.burst and ss.relic.doubleyolk all say so. What keeps it from reading as a pupil is that nothing around it is regular. The hide is a rust body under a warmer lit face pushed up-left, hand-stitched along the lower seam in ochre dashes. Deep $rust is otherwise only an accent in this library, so a body this size in it belongs to nothing else on the shelf, and the amber crowd (ss.relic.mine, ss.relic.digest) stays clear.",
  "tags": ["ss-relic"],
  "size": [32, 32],
